//!
//! # Rank
//!
//! The rank of every kernel lock, in one place.
//!
//! ## Overview
//!
//! A CPU takes spinlocks in rising rank order, and a thread takes mutexes in
//! rising rank order (`Arch::Spin`, `Sched::Mutex`, concept doc 24). The two
//! orders are separate: a mutex is never taken while a spinlock is held, so
//! every mutex comes before every spinlock anyway.
//!
//! A rank sits above the ranks of every lock that may be held when it is
//! taken. The chains that decide the order:
//!
//! ```text
//!   spinlocks
//!   mutex guard (20) ──► run queue (60)              waking a waiter
//!   heap (70) ──► page tables (75) ──► frames (80)   the heap grows
//!   anything ──► console (90) ──► serial (91)        printing
//!
//!   mutexes
//!   process table (10) ──► VFS (30) ──► address space (40)
//!     launch: table, then the slot's regions (reset, adoptImage)
//!     a copy to user memory under VFS faults: VFS, then the space
//!     resolve of a file page without VFS: drop the space, take VFS, retake
//! ```
//!
//! Gaps are left so a new lock fits between two existing ones.

/// Spinlock ranks.
namespace Lib::Rank {
  /// The guard spinlock inside every `Sched::Mutex`.
  const MUTEX_GUARD: u64 = 20;

  /// The spinlock of a subsystem's wait queue.
  const WAIT_QUEUE: u64 = 30;

  /// The scheduler's run queue and thread states.
  const RUN_QUEUE: u64 = 60;

  /// The kernel heap.
  const HEAP: u64 = 70;

  /// Every page table edit, kernel and user.
  const PAGE_TABLES: u64 = 75;

  /// The physical frame allocator.
  const FRAMES: u64 = 80;

  /// The framebuffer console.
  const CONSOLE: u64 = 90;

  /// The serial port.
  const SERIAL: u64 = 91;
}

/// Mutex ranks.
namespace Lib::MutexRank {
  /// The process table.
  const PROCESS_TABLE: u64 = 10;

  /// The whole VFS tree, every descriptor table and every current directory.
  /// A per-process descriptor lock would rank between the two once processes
  /// have several threads.
  const VFS: u64 = 30;

  /// A process's address space and region table, one per process slot
  /// (`Proc::Memory`), shared by the threads of the process. A page fault
  /// takes it, also inside a system call that holds the VFS lock.
  const ADDRESS_SPACE: u64 = 40;
}
